Install Paramiko module:
Ubuntu :
-> apt-get install python-paramiko
SLES/RHEL :
-> SLES: Install python-devel, python-setuptools
-> RHEL: Install python-devel.ppc64le and python-setuptools.noarch
-> Download pycrypto-2.6.1.tar.gz & paramiko-1.16.0.tar.gz
-> For pycrypto : python setup.py build & python setup.py install
-> For paramiko : easy_install ./
-> Then import paramiko.

RHEL :
-> yum install python-paramiko.noarch

Please setup repo for rsct and DynamicRM packages that are required
for dlpar, script will take care of installation part.

NOTE: The vNIC must already be added to the LPAR manually before
running this test. This script does NOT provision the vNIC itself.

Explanation of the input parameters:
hmc_pwd ---> HMC password
hmc_username ---> HMC user name
manageSystem ---> Provide the HMC managed system name of the CEC
slot_num ---> Virtual slot number on which the vNIC interface exists.
              Should be 3 - 2999. Space separated for multiple vNICs.
mac_id ---> MAC address of the vNIC interface. Space separated if
            multiple. Example: "02:03:03:03:03:01"
device_ip ---> IP address to be configured on the vNIC interface after
               DLPAR. Space separated if multiple.
netmasks ---> Netmask value for the vNIC interface. Space separated
              if multiple. Example: "255.255.255.0"
peer_ip ---> Peer IP to ping after DLPAR to verify backend traffic.
             Space separated if multiple.
sriov_adapters ---> Space separated physical location code (DRC) of
                    the SRIOV backing adapter(s) assigned to the
                    hypervisor. Used to resolve adapter_id from HMC.
                    Example: "U78DA.ND0.WZS0027-P0-C11"
sriov_ports ---> Space separated port number(s) of the SRIOV backing
                 adapter(s). Example: "0" or "0 1"
num_of_dlpar ---> Number of DLPAR remove/add cycles to perform per
                  slot. Default: 1
lockdown_enable ---> Set to True to enable kernel lockdown before
                     running DLPAR in test_vnic_dlpar_lockdown.
                     Default: False
lockdown_mode ---> Kernel lockdown mode to activate when
                   lockdown_enable is True.
                   Accepted values: integrity, confidentiality
                   Default: integrity
                   Note: lockdown is a one-way escalation at runtime
                   (none->integrity->confidentiality). Downgrade to
                   'none' requires a reboot.

Example Usage:
avocado run vnic_dlpar.py -m vnic_dlpar.py.data/vnic_dlpar.yaml
avocado run vnic_dlpar.py:VnicDlpar.test_vnic_dlpar -m vnic_dlpar.py.data/vnic_dlpar.yaml
avocado run vnic_dlpar.py:VnicDlpar.test_vnic_dlpar_lockdown -m vnic_dlpar.py.data/vnic_dlpar.yaml
